# Symbols a statically-linked gl4es client must expose so libx11-compat can find
# them via dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT) at runtime (see src/glx.c). Fed to the macOS
# linker as -exported_symbols_list, this is the ONLY thing the demo exports: it
# hides the ~979 public gl* and all gl4es internals that the static link would
# otherwise dump into the binary's global symbol table, and (as export roots,
# alongside the executable entry point) lets -dead_strip drop the unreachable
# code with them.
#
# initialize_gl4es and set_getprocaddress wire the layer, and glFlush drains
# gl4es's batch before the composite readback. glReadPixels and glPixelStorei are
# taken from the provider (ANGLE), not the client, so are not listed. Leading
# underscores are the Mach-O symbol spelling.
#
# NewGLState/ActivateGLState/DeleteGLState let libx11-compat give each GLXContext
# its own gl4es glstate and switch it at glXMakeCurrent. Without them, gl4es falls
# back to a single global glstate whose GLES program/VBO/texture handles are bound
# to whichever EGLContext was current when they were built; a second, non-sharing
# context (mesa multictx, sharedtex) then draws with handles invalid in its own
# EGLContext and renders nothing. dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT) only sees exported symbols,
# so they must be listed here for the per-context switch to engage.
#
# Limitation, by design: because only these are exported, glXGetProcAddress in
# libx11-compat (which resolves gl* via dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT)) cannot hand a demo
# its own statically-linked gl* aliases. The mesa xdemos call gl* directly (bound
# at link time), so this never bites them; a client that instead resolved gl*
# through glXGetProcAddress would need those symbols exported too.
_initialize_gl4es
_set_getprocaddress
_glFlush
_NewGLState
_ActivateGLState
_DeleteGLState
